The intervals OVERLAP on [0.675651, 0.678674] — narrowly, but they overlap, so the difference
is NOT established. +3.19% on the harness statistic; +3.77% on ratio-of-medians; the two agree
in sign and magnitude this time, which is better than the last two attempts managed.

The raw arms: our p50 fell 7.99% (18.279 -> 16.817 ms) while SciPy's fell 4.52%
(11.980 -> 11.438 ms). SciPy's arm is the host control, so roughly 3.5% is attributable and the
rest is a faster host. That is consistent with the counted picture and with the +3.19%.

AND IT IS STILL AN ACROSS-RUN COMPARISON. Four A/A nulls certify each run internally; nothing
spans the two. The narrow overlap plus the two statistics agreeing is the strongest form this
comparison has taken, and it still falls short of separation.

## WHAT THE COUNTS SAY THE PACKED WORK ACTUALLY DID

  NativeSparseLu::solve, Ir attributed to the function, DEFAULT convection path:

    jagged (pre-packing)          869,689,818
    packed, indexed (landed)    1,265,173,225    +45.5%   <- a regression, shipped briefly
    packed + zip (28f9ee270)      890,761,337    -29.6% vs indexed, +2.4% vs jagged

So on instruction count the packed layout is a wash against jagged; what this session actually
did on the solve was find and remove a 45% regression that had landed. The wall-clock row above
is consistent with "no worse, possibly slightly better", which is what a layout change that
trades instructions for locality should look like — and neither number is strong enough to
claim the locality win.

## WHAT IS STILL ON THE TABLE, sized

  * The factor now pays an O(nnz) conversion: `PackedTriangularRows::from_rows` copies every
    entry out of the jagged rows AFTER elimination. Emitting packed rows directly from
    `factorize_csr` removes that copy entirely. It is the largest single un-taken item on this
    cell and it is a real rewrite, not a micro-lever.
  * The solve's remaining per-element cost is ~19 Ir/element-update against an irreducible core
    of maybe 6-8 (load column, load value, gather `y[col]`, multiply-subtract). The gather's
    bounds check is not removable in safe Rust; the rest is loop and iterator machinery.

